# onegw — Docker Compose settings.
#
# Copy to .env and edit, then `docker compose up -d`.
# Prefer one command? `./scripts/docker_deploy.sh --compose` writes this file for
# you with a minted gateway key (0600) and never rotates an existing one.
#
# .env is read twice by Compose: for docker-compose.yml interpolation (the host
# knobs below) and, through `env_file`, as the gateway container's environment —
# so everything you add here is visible to the running gateway without a rebuild.
# It is gitignored because it holds secrets.

# --- host side (Compose) ------------------------------------------------------
# Published host port. 8080 is onegw's own default, so on a machine already
# running a gateway it is taken — and Docker does not always fail loudly: on
# macOS (Colima / Docker Desktop) the container comes up "healthy" while
# 127.0.0.1:8080 keeps answering from the HOST process, which looks like a
# wrong-password failure. Pick a free port if in doubt; `docker compose up -d`
# recreates the container after a change here.
ONEGW_HOST_PORT=8080

# 127.0.0.1 keeps the dashboard loopback-only. 0.0.0.0 (the default) publishes on
# every interface, which is exactly why ONEGW_KEYS below is mandatory.
ONEGW_HOST_BIND=0.0.0.0

# Image to run. `--compose --build` builds this tree and sets this to onegw:compose.
ONEGW_IMAGE=ghcr.io/freepeak/onegw:latest

# Name behind `docker exec -it onegw …` (defaults to onegw).
# ONEGW_CONTAINER_NAME=onegw
# Tag stamped into a locally built binary's `onegw version`.
# ONEGW_BUILD_VERSION=dev

# --- gateway credentials ------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED, and never auto-generated by the image: comma-separated client keys,
# sent by clients as `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Generate one with
# `openssl rand -hex 24`. Without it the gateway refuses to serve 0.0.0.0 and
# exits — see `docker compose logs onegw`.
# ONEGW_KEYS=

# Dashboard / admin API password. Left unset, first boot mints a random one,
# prints it once as "FIRST-RUN ADMIN PASSWORD" and persists it at
# /data/admin_password (0600) on the data volume. Set it to choose your own;
# change it later from the dashboard (Settings → Admin password).
# ONEGW_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- upstream provider keys ---------------------------------------------------
# ONEGW_PROVIDER_<NAME>_KEY, <NAME> = provider name uppercased, dashes folded to
# underscores. _KEY2 … _KEY9 add rotating accounts to the same provider. The
# dashboard's Providers page writes keys into the config volume instead — use
# whichever of the two you want to keep in version control.
# ONEGW_PROVIDER_OPENROUTER_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
# ONEGW_PROVIDER_OPENCODE_KEY=...
# ONEGW_PROVIDER_XAI_KEY=xai-...

# --- gateway behaviour --------------------------------------------------------
# ONEGW_TASK_ROUTING=on          # task-aware combo reordering
# ONEGW_DATA_DIR=/data           # already set by the image
# ONEGW_CONFIG=/etc/onegw/onegw.toml
